{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland","id":1516631,"title":"Rage in Scotland","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-29T04:32:48-07:00","id":1516631,"title":"Rage in Scotland","content":"So I've been running this game in Scotland (white howler renewal) and by reading many posts here and documenting myself I'm starting to slowly flesh out the country on the supernatural level. Here is more or less the things I've thought about, or starting to think about implementing. <br /> <br /> I like the idea of Scotland being a sort of liminal country, thinking of it a bit like the Egypt of the UK where the supernatural is pretty much alive in the thoughts of people, especially locals like Highlanders that would for example keep putting some milk outside their windows for the fairies and such. They consider everything &quot;magical-ish&quot; to be fairy or ghost related. Umbra habitants have an easier time crossing the veil/gauntlet everywhere in the country.<br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> I've created a map where I document everything that could be of interest so: all pictish stones sites, stone circles, some burials sites, radiocative wastes/spillage, faery/ghosts sites. I also added official mentions of caerns, pits and other WoD things. Though since I don't really read a lot of non-werewolf books I may have missed a lot of information except for Changelin/fae since they have a specific supplement for the british isles.<br /> <br /> <b>Werewolf:</b> The country is mainly populated by Fianna and Fenris, most caern are mono tribes caern with a few exceptions. Every caern is in serious distress with the BSD campaign to get back their country, a few caern have been lost over the years. I only 5 caerns for gaian garou, against 8 pits (some being ancient caerns). I may be looking to add one or two more gaian caern for the fianna and/or fenris. I don't have much in the highlands, and I'm thinking of the feroe islands for the fenris since the shetland is an absolute wyrm hell with the whole pretrol thing in sullom voe.<br /> The big idea is to keep the werewolves here under constant pressure, they don't know when they will be hit, they just know they will.<br /> <br /> The &quot;belt&quot;on the south with glasgow and edimbourg is a very active place for BSD, with the caern of gloom and sorrow that fell, the Demborough/Damburrow location that is supposedly located around. I've read a bit about the pretanic order and I'm thinking about taking them back to scotland.<br /> Fera: Most mention of Ceilican in old books are how they got decimated and/or turned to the wyrm with one of their abandonned/fallen taghairm located in the highlands. I haven't thought too much about it, but since I want them to be present in the game I may give them back this caern and/or add another one somewhere. I'm thinking that the fay-cat could be a way to introduce not only a local fera (and we don't have much of those in EU) but a way to introduce some changelin/fae (some of my player are slowly turning their head in this direction) elements as well.<br /> <br /> <b>Wraith:</b> I really want to give Scotland a special touch with ghosts, so I've adapted some things from rage across Egypt. Sometimes a place become &quot;touched&quot; by death and anyone being there or walking there can have a hint of the shadowlands and it's ghosts or worse end up there (and die mostly), it also work the other way around allowing ghost to pass while it's open. It's a phenomenon that is random and can appear anywhere though most often in places that are &quot;predisposed&quot; (so related to death by folklore or where people died and such). <br /> - I plan to use the Cororuc ghost as a bridge between the pcs and wraiths if they manage to find him and persuade him to be an helper of their cause.<br /> <br /> <b>Fae/Changelin:</b> They are supposedly all over the place I guess, I definitely have to look a bit more into it. I know that the old and super powerful faes were gone, and then kinda returned and I wans thinking to exploit this so my player could get more context of the old Scotland/caledonia and maybe get some informations to the white howler tribes for creature that have actually know them (not necessarily on good terms though).<br /> <br /> <b>Vampire:</b> I read here quite some interesting information about the Dunsirn (I think it was initiated by Blackfox) and I want to add them in my scottish setting. My player don't spent much time in cities, so I initially didn't think too hard about vampires, but I have a few very filthy rich characters (one that actually have wealth from the scottish petrol industry, haha) so the Dunsirn could definitely come into play at some point.<br /> <br /> <b>Mage:</b> I don't know much about mage so I probably have missed a lot of things. I've read some things here and there from supplement like VDA british isles but I don't have much for my contemporary setting. I know wyrm-affiliated mages are doing shenanigans in the shetland (sullom voe) but that's about it more or less, and the shit going there seems really huge.<br /> <br /> <b>Hunter: </b>I really don't know much about those but with such a &quot;supernaturally active&quot; country, it has to have some real hunter there.<br /> <br /> Don't hesitate to tell me if you have used Scotland, or scottish related things in your game, or if you things that could be added !","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8944-tanakyelle","name":"Tanakyelle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8944_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T07:55:42-07:00","id":1516649,"title":"","content":"A few thoughts:<br /> The difference with Scotland could be the presence of the Lochs, most of them have monster legends (not just Loch Ness) and one of the native supernatural predators is the Water Horse or Kelpie, which could well be a Fey Nightmare from the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> The Lochs themselves could be the source of a lower gauntlet (essentially large portals) as well as the Highlands being difficult to settle, it\u2019s one of the largest wildernesses in the UK.<br /> <br /> There are around 10 Ley lines that run across Scotland the Sickle Ley running from Tintagel through Stonehenge and then up into the highlands, so the place could be abundant with places of power (smaller fonts, glades and wells as well as Cairns).<br /> <br /> The lines could lower the gauntlet or could be a result of the gauntlet, but with this much power it would explain why a largely remote area is of interest to Mage, Fey as well as Garou.<br /> <br /> Mage would like be Druidic or pagan, with more hermetic or technological in the southern cities, local nephandi might well be derived from BSD kinfolk with unpleasant alliances.<br /> However if their is a contest for fonts or the Ley lines there may be rival Faustian nephandus who serve demons rather than the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> This dosen\u2019t help the Garou it just puts them under more pressure as they have competing forces trying to claim their caerns.<br /> <br /> Formori could be common from the BSD pits as well as other wyrmholes, the Romans used armies of formori to annihilate the Howler kinfolk so you could have some ancient breeds lurking in the Highlands.<br /> <br /> Similar to Nordic countries where they still leave offering in faery homes outside homes and beside roads, older Scot\u2019s couldn\u2019t help but be affected by the lower gauntlet on a subconscious level let alone staying in at night.<br /> So they\u2019d definitely leave offerings and observe small rituals just in case.<br /> <br /> Ley Lines were also called Corpse Lines by some paranaturalists, so they may also affect the Shroud, this makes sense as Scotland has a huge number of haunted locations with Glamis Castle being one of the most haunted places in the world.<br /> <br /> BSD activity would be high if only to keep an eye on lost Howlers, in my mind Scotland has lots of old families and bloodlines so it\u2019s a likely place for a child of lion to emerge from a lost line.<br /> <br /> Finally for all of the above it could also be a stronghold for Seelie and Unseelie Fey, if it is a place of power there would be ancient and still strong freeholds (Fey caerns) hidden from mortal eyes, if mortals still have a semi belief this reduces the weavers affects on the Fey (being creatures of magic), plus you could have a fey true fey tucked away who didn\u2019t cloak themselves in human flesh to escape the approach of science.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T08:33:35-07:00","id":1516654,"title":"","content":"A few thoughts:<br /> The difference with Scotland could be the presence of the Lochs, most of them have monster legends (not just Loch Ness) and one of the native supernatural predators is the Water Horse or Kelpie, which could well be a Fey Nightmare from the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> The Lochs themselves could be the source of a lower gauntlet (essentially large portals) as well as the Highlands being difficult to settle, it\u2019s one of the largest wildernesses in the UK.<br /> <br /> There are around 10 Ley lines that run across Scotland the Sickle Ley running from Tintagel through Stonehenge and then up into the highlands, so the place could be abundant with places of power (smaller fonts, glades and wells as well as Cairns).<br /> <br /> The lines could lower the gauntlet or could be a result of the gauntlet, but with this much power it would explain why a largely remote area is of interest to Mage, Fey as well as Garou.<br /> <br /> Mage would like be Druidic or pagan, with more hermetic or technological in the southern cities, local nephandi might well be derived from BSD kinfolk with unpleasant alliances.<br /> However if their is a contest for fonts or the Ley lines there may be rival Faustian nephandus who serve demons rather than the Wyrm.<br /> <br /> This dosen\u2019t help the Garou it just puts them under more pressure as they have competing forces trying to claim their caerns.<br /> <br /> Formori could be common from the BSD pits as well as other wyrmholes, the Romans used armies of formori to annihilate the Howler kinfolk so you could have some ancient breeds lurking in the Highlands.<br /> <br /> Similar to Nordic countries where they still leave offering in faery homes outside homes and beside roads, older Scot\u2019s couldn\u2019t help but be affected by the lower gauntlet on a subconscious level let alone staying in at night.<br /> So they\u2019d definitely leave offerings and observe small rituals just in case.<br /> <br /> Ley Lines were also called Corpse Lines by some paranaturalists, so they may also affect the Shroud, this makes sense as Scotland has a huge number of haunted locations with Glamis Castle being one of the most haunted places in the world.<br /> <br /> BSD activity would be high if only to keep an eye on lost Howlers, in my mind Scotland has lots of old families and bloodlines so it\u2019s a likely place for a child of lion to emerge from a lost line.<br /> <br /> Finally for all of the above it could also be a stronghold for Seelie and Unseelie Fey, if it is a place of power there would be ancient and still strong freeholds (Fey caerns) hidden from mortal eyes, if mortals still have a semi belief this reduces the weavers affects on the Fey (being creatures of magic), plus you could have a fey true fey tucked away who didn\u2019t cloak themselves in human flesh to escape the approach of science. zz0.rd1wbeotwdzz","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T09:49:02-07:00","id":1516665,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland#post1516631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Vampire:</b> I read here quite some interesting information about the Dunsirn (I think it was initiated by Blackfox) and I want to add them in my scottish setting. My player don't spent much time in cities, so I initially didn't think too hard about vampires, but I have a few very filthy rich characters (one that actually have wealth from the scottish petrol industry, haha) so the Dunsirn could definitely come into play at some point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Since you mentioned them, <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Dunsirn\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>'s a quick compilation of official Dunsirn facts by the wiki.<br /> <br /> Imagery-wise, references that come to mind are <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sawney_Bean\" target=\"_blank\">the Bean clan</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christie-Cleek\" target=\"_blank\">Christie Cleek</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweeney_Todd\" target=\"_blank\">Sweeney Todd</a> (yes, i know he was a londoner, details), <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shylock#In_the_play\" target=\"_blank\">Shylock</a>, for another clearly not scottish source of inspiration and dramatic beats (specially for the occasional Dunsirn veiled resentment of Giovanni imposition on them and inroads of payback)\u200b, among <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colnbrook#History\" target=\"_blank\">others</a> and\u200b, for a considerably more benign take of their lot,<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ebenezer_Scrooge\" target=\"_blank\"> Scrooge</a>. No, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrooge_McDuck\" target=\"_blank\">not that one</a> (though he's a definite scot). <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> The wiki brings up the possibility of the family being either of Fianna or BSD kinfolk in origin. Though as an aside i dare say Glass Walker - or Bone Gnawer, who do have a bad rep with a story of occasional cannibalism - might work quite well for twists. Or maybe they don't know themselves anymore due to generations of estrangement and association with the Giovanni.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516654#post1516654\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A few thoughts:<br /> The difference with Scotland could be the presence of the Lochs, most of them have monster legends (not just Loch Ness) and one of the native supernatural predators is the Water Horse or Kelpie, which could well be a Fey Nightmare from the Dreaming.<br /> <br /> The Lochs themselves could be the source of a lower gauntlet (essentially large portals) as well as the Highlands being difficult to settle, it\u2019s one of the largest wildernesses in the UK.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Speaking of the lochs, wouldn't the possible presence of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Will-o%27-the-wisp\" target=\"_blank\">will-'o-wisps</a> on them be noteworthy too? What would they be, visitors from the Umbra, Underworld, Dreaming, all of the above or neither? It also crossed my mind that while more of a Northern England phenomenon, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barghest\" target=\"_blank\">Barghests</a> and other shadowy black dogs legends might be of interest exploring from a Garou or other supernatural perspective.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 06-29-2023, 01:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T12:35:24-07:00","id":1516681,"title":"","content":"My thread on the Dunsirn can be found <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1464311-the-dunsirn-as-a-setting-element-in-werewolf\">here</a> if you only remember it, but don't know its location.<br /> <br /> With the relatively small size and population of Scotland, and with the large presence of Garou relative to the people, I'd concentrate vampires almost exclusively in a few cities. If you want to keep to canon, the Toreador are the dominant clan and have some allies among the Brujah because they oppose the Ventrue in England. Ventrue would be one of the rarer clans. And I'd probably limit the Tremere to one chantry in Edinburgh as explained in this <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1493307-gehenna-cults-of-elysium#post1494104\">post</a> concerning the Gehenna cult known as the Ninth Wave. You might have a few Gangrel or Nosferatu hiding in the Scottish wilderness, either keeping their head down to avoid the lupines or being some kind of hag known in folklore (the Cailleach is suppose to dwell somewhere in Argyll and Bute, particulalry around the mountain of Ben Nevis).<br /> <br /> My recommendation for Changeling is that unless your players really want the specifics of the Changeling setting, it is just better to go by the actual folklore and fairy legends of Scotland and the British Isles. I am not a big fan of the Changeling setting since it is the worst of all the gamelines at emulating the source material (either folklore or pop culture). It's really is its own thing which I don't think fills the expectation of most people (though I recognize that game does have its own devoted fans). If you want to do research for inspiration, I can recommend this <a href=\"https://britishfairies.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">blog</a>. For the purposes of a Werewolf game, I'd treat them like they were their own special type of &quot;spirit&quot; in a section of the Umbra all its own who frequently materialize in places strong in the Wyld. With the decline of the Wyld in the world, the &quot;inhabitable&quot; places of the world for fairies have greatly declined and will continue to do so.<br /> <br /> If I was going to include hunters, they'd be inspired more by Solomon Kane than the Catholic Inquisition because of Scotland's Presbyterianism.<br /> <br /> If you like to read, you may want to check out the Terror Tales series, which is a British series of new horror fiction short story anthologies set in different regions. They have two books set in Scotland, the <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Terror-Tales-Scottish-Lowlands-Finch-ebook/dp/B09JMW67JX/ref=sr_1_1?keywords=terror+tales+of+scotland&amp;s=books&amp;sr=1-1\" target=\"_blank\">Lowlands</a> and the <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/TERROR-TALES-SCOTTISH-HIGHLANDS-Finch/dp/1906331995/ref=sr_1_5?keywords=terror+tales+of+scotland&amp;s=books&amp;sr=1-5\" target=\"_blank\">Highlands</a>. I personally find the actual short stories hit and miss (typical for any new fiction anthology), but each book has multiple stories (typically two pages only) of actual local myths, legends, and folklore before each story, and they are fantastic. They'd be a great way to spice up your setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T13:17:43-07:00","id":1516683,"title":"","content":"<b>Wraiths:</b> Garou usually don't become Wraiths, but apparently it happens for some tainted werewolves. This would open up a connection between the BSDs and local Spectres, but maybe there is room for a Howler Wraith NPC who is on a redemption and penance arc. An unexpected ally like that could be interesting.<br /> <br /> <b>Vampires:</b> If you want to stay away from cities, Gangrel are of course a choice, but it would also be a chance to use a lone Lhiannan holdover. You could for example import and adapt <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Magda\" target=\"_blank\">Magda</a> from Ireland.<br /> <br /> <b>Mages:</b> If I remember correctly, Scotland used to be Verbena and Dreamspeaker country before they got into a tussle with the Inquisition. Now it's pretty contested territory between Traditions and Technocrats.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T13:23:22-07:00","id":1516684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516665#post1516665\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speaking of the lochs, wouldn't the possible presence of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Will-o%27-the-wisp\" target=\"_blank\">will-'o-wisps</a> on them be noteworthy too? What would they be, visitors from the Umbra, Underworld, Dreaming, all of the above or neither? It also crossed my mind that while more of a Northern England phenomenon, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barghest\" target=\"_blank\">Barghests</a> and other shadowy black dogs legends might be of interest exploring from a Garou or other supernatural perspective.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wisps are nasty creatures folklore suggests they lead travellers into marshes where they never find their way out, or are drained of life (so a vampiric creature).<br /> <br /> Could be a creature of the dreaming, umbra or from the Tempest, to be truthful I like to have hybrids - death spirits that frequent the dark umbra, and hybrid spirit/chimera.<br /> <br /> The black or green dogs of the highlands are the C\u00f9-s\u00ecth, so they could be BSD kinfolk or something else entirely, again they are Fey hounds according to legend.<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T13:41:27-07:00","id":1516686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516683#post1516683\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Wraiths:</b> Garou usually don't become Wraiths, but apparently it happens for some tainted werewolves. This would open up a connection between the BSDs and local Spectres, but maybe there is room for a Howler Wraith NPC who is on a redemption and penance arc. An unexpected ally like that could be interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see Garou heavily orientated to the dark umbra such as the Ivory Priesthood or Silent Striders becoming Wraiths as well. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516683#post1516683\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Vampires:</b> If you want to stay away from cities, Gangrel are of course a choice, but it would also be a chance to use a lone Lhiannan holdover. You could for example import and adapt <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Magda\" target=\"_blank\">Magda</a> from Ireland.</div> </div> </div> </div>Theres an old Valkyrie Magazine article (Britain by Night) which features the Danu bloodline, these are Changelings that have become a Fey bloodline after the embrace (which could work quite well).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516683#post1516683\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Mages:</b> If I remember correctly, Scotland used to be Verbena and Dreamspeaker country before they got into a tussle with the Inquisition. Now it's pretty contested territory between Traditions and Technocrats.</div> </div> </div> </div>I can see the Order of Hermes in Edinburgh, and possibly Death Druids from the Euthanatous as well.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-29T23:24:54-07:00","id":1516736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516684#post1516684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Wisps are nasty creatures folklore suggests they lead travellers into marshes where they never find their way out, or are drained of life (so a vampiric creature).<br /> <br /> Could be a creature of the dreaming, umbra or from the Tempest, to be truthful I like to have hybrids - death spirits that frequent the dark umbra, and hybrid spirit/chimera.\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I thought of them as more malicious than vampiric, but now that you brought it up, i can't stop thinking of <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sgTMnaEvTAc\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>.<br /> What makes me think of a demented master of <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Puppetry\" target=\"_blank\">Puppetry</a>, some sort of spectre-risen or maybe some obscure thing taken from <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Hunter:_The_Walking_Dead\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516684#post1516684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The black or green dogs of the highlands are the C\u00f9-s\u00ecth, so they could be BSD kinfolk or something else entirely, again they are Fey hounds according to legend.<br /> <br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I can imagine some Howler and/or Fianna kinfolk trying to <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Moliate\" target=\"_blank\">Moliate</a> themselves into something dog- or wolf-like to be closer to their relatives (human, Garou, animal or fae) and some bizarre things coming up from that depending on circunstances.<br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516683#post1516683\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Vampires:</b> If you want to stay away from cities, Gangrel are of course a choice, but it would also be a chance to use a lone Lhiannan holdover. You could for example import and adapt <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Magda\" target=\"_blank\">Magda</a> from Ireland.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not meaning to rain on other people's parade, but my thoughts on Magda are...<br /> <br /> &quot;Ain't Magda short for Magdalene? Is it an irish/celtic/proto-celtic name?\u200b&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Rhymes with Dagda.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Good enough.&quot;\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> Ok, trying to be more serious now. Magda has some issues as a NPC for me - for one, her background feels an awful lot like a rip-off from Baba Yaga in Rage Across Russia, but with &quot;maiden/wild woman&quot; in place of &quot;crone/hag&quot;, along with a different clan/bloodline for paper-thin disguise.<br /> <br /> For another, well, she's pretty much the only canon Lhiannan NPC ever written, feels kind of very lazy to just port her from an isle to the other - might as well write one's own i'd say, or come up with some Moon Bridge-like power (what might be decent use for the fact her Ogham <i>is two levels higher than <b>all </b>canon powers of the discipline</i>) for the OG Magda to pop up between countries <i>and handwave usual limits the bloodline weakness should put on her movements</i>.<br /> <br /> <br /> And yeah, there's the whole &quot;5th gen from a bloodline that supposedly tops at 7th generation&quot; thing, but that is such a bizarrely off-the wall thing for a writer to do that i see as more strength than weakness, as trying to explore the issue could lead a ST to all kinds of awesomely mad ideas. Though an easy way out of all issues would be to say &quot;Lhiannan are what the Tzimisce call themselves in the Isles/celtic remnant enclaves&quot;, make Ogham into a branch of Koldunic Sorcery and leave at that. The Tzimisce weakness and discipline set are close enough for it to work nicely in my past experience.\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T00:05:17-07:00","id":1516738,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516736#post1516736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not meaning to rain on other people's parade, but my thoughts on Magda are...\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree! Hence why I said to adapt her. I think Lhiannan are one of the bloodlines every ST has a homebrew version of, because as written there are some problems (Ogham as koldunic sorcery is something I agree on). If we recast them as Tzimisce bloodline, I would maybe have their Vicissitude work on nearby plants instead of flesh and replace the Level 1 Ogham power with something else. Depending on how much she's going to be used that might not be worth the effort, though.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T06:05:17-07:00","id":1516765,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516738#post1516738\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree! Hence why I said to adapt her. I think Lhiannan are one of the bloodlines every ST has a homebrew version of, because as written there are some problems (Ogham as koldunic sorcery is something I agree on). If we recast them as Tzimisce bloodline, I would maybe have their Vicissitude work on nearby plants instead of flesh and replace the Level 1 Ogham power with something else. Depending on how much she's going to be used that might not be worth the effort, though.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, if one really wants to lean onto a &quot;druid as misanthrope hobo priest of the green&quot; D&amp;D vibe perhaps - and even then i might simply port the &quot;Of Rose &amp; Thorn&quot; merit from Requiem that tweaks Animalism instead - otherwise Tzimisce-as-is, with Ogham as one of the Koldunic Ways and Vicissitude to &quot;fomorize&quot; one's retainers, herd and/or allies as need be is completely fine, imho.\u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> As an aside, <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Daughters_of_Cacophony\" target=\"_blank\">Daughters of Cacophony</a> with crazed romantic leanings and a taste for seclusion wandering the countryside as Banshee-like beings can certainly be a thing too. That said, <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Melpominee\" target=\"_blank\">their</a> discipline is the kind of thing that could easily be retooled in one or more discipline combo powers. But then i'm a strong believer that the &quot;every new trick or power needs to be a signature discipline/bloodline or thaumaturgy path&quot; mindset on Masquerade needs to go away.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1147,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","user":"Baaldam","date":". 06-30-2023, 09:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T07:01:07-07:00","id":1516771,"title":"","content":"I will mention that, as far as Wraith goes, one of the chapters in the Book of Haunts was about a Scottish castle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T07:37:03-07:00","id":1516773,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516736#post1516736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I thought of them as more malicious than vampiric, but now that you brought it up, i can't stop thinking of <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sgTMnaEvTAc\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>.<br /> What makes me think of a demented master of <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Puppetry\" target=\"_blank\">Puppetry</a>, some sort of spectre-risen or maybe some obscure thing taken from <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Hunter:_The_Walking_Dead\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely if my memories correct it was their luring people to their deaths in the marshes, but Lifeforce is such a good cheesy film.<br /> <br /> I can see them haunting the tempest marshes and preying on plasms and wraiths, but passing across the weak shroud of the Lochs where they can do the same to the living.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516736#post1516736\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can imagine some Howler and/or Fianna kinfolk trying to <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Moliate\" target=\"_blank\">Moliate</a> themselves into something dog- or wolf-like to be closer to their relatives (human, Garou, animal or fae) and some bizarre things coming up from that depending on circunstances.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bVery nice, especially if you use the Forsaken Wolf Blooded Kinfolk to represent strong bloodlines as per No One of Consequences Homebrew.<br /> I can see kinfolk wraiths capitalising on Moliate to give them the power they lacked in life.<br /> <br /> <b>Magda</b>: I agree in that she is a little limited as written, even from an Irish Celtic perspective it\u2019s missing the concept of the Morrigan and the triple Goddess.<br /> <br /> Thats without developing a native Pictish Kindred, Mithras is technically one of many foreign invaders albeit he arrived a very long time ago, so it stands to reason that their may be resident ancient kindred .<br /> <br /> Although the Scots eventually entered a Union with the English it was through Royal succession with previous military campaigns spilling lots of blood but never really sticking (this goes back to the Roman occupation ala Hadrians Wall).<br /> <br /> The Toreador would never have had sufficient power to hold Mithras off (if he\u2019d really wanted to take Edinburgh) unless they had ancient support, so perhaps their is an older Pictish Kindred sleeping in the Highlands that Mithras avoided challenging too greatly given his more local issues within the English Baronies, Sabbat incursions and the Tremere.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T07:37:53-07:00","id":1516775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland#post1516631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Vampire:</b> I read here quite some interesting information about the Dunsirn (I think it was initiated by Blackfox) and I want to add them in my scottish setting. My player don't spent much time in cities, so I initially didn't think too hard about vampires, but I have a few very filthy rich characters (one that actually have wealth from the scottish petrol industry, haha) so the Dunsirn could definitely come into play at some point.</div> </div> </div> </div>A lot of the initial stuff about Scotland and the rest of the UK was first established in the A World of Darkness sourcebook (and its 2nd ed), and later expanded on in Dark Ages: Britain. The main thing is that the lowlands around the border have long been dominated by the Toreador, who viewed Mithras of London as a rival. The rural areas and north were more the domain of Gangrel (including ancient Celts/Picts, medieval Vikings, and more modern clan types), but probably also some of the other &quot;Animism&quot; clans like the Nosferatu.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Mage:</b> I don't know much about mage so I probably have missed a lot of things. I've read some things here and there from supplement like VDA british isles but I don't have much for my contemporary setting. I know wyrm-affiliated mages are doing shenanigans in the shetland (sullom voe) but that's about it more or less, and the shit going there seems really huge. </div> </div> </div>In the modern era, besides the modern British Technocracy, the most prominent faction is likely to be the Verbena, both of the traditional Celtic type and the Nordic influenced hold overs. (What in the Dark Ages would've been the Old Faith and the Valdaerman, respectively.) Also the more modern Wiccan and New Age types. <br /> <br /> Those same Celtic and Nordic influences also produce Euthanatos. Mainly ones concerned with issues of fate/destiny, sacred oaths, watching over certain communities/villages/towns, and eliminating threats.<br /> <br /> The Order of Hermes has a long presence in Britain, including parts of Scotland. House Diedne was one of the founding Houses, mixing Druidism with Hermetic Arts, but were wiped out by the rest of the Order during the early Middle Ages (mostly because of Tremere shenanigans in order to distract from their vampire experiments). Some of them may have escaped into the Fey realms. In some of the Ars Magica books, House Ex Miscelania was presented as having a very strong presence in Scotland, especially with those incorporating &quot;witchcraft&quot; and &quot;shamanism&quot;. <br /> <br /> A lot of the Cult of Ecstasy activity in Scotland likely originated with the Romantic period, especially with groups like the Fellowship of Pan. Then there's the more modern types, especially in the major cities. <br /> <br /> Really, all of the Traditions, as well as the Hollow Ones, would be represented in modern Scotland. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Hunter: </b>I really don't know much about those but with such a &quot;supernaturally active&quot; country, it has to have some real hunter there. </div> </div> </div>Do you mean witchhunters like the Inquisition and the Arcanum, or the Imbued from the original Hunter: The Reckoning?<br /> <br /> Protestant religious hunters are certainly going to be there. Also an Arcanum house in at least one of the major cities. And the British government probably has its own counterpart to the FBI's special affairs division. <br /> <br /> Hunter: The Reckoning had a couple of prominent British characters, mostly in the material written by Adam Tinsworth (who was from Scotland). The Wayward creed book and the Moonstruck book about werebeasts, and at least a few other books that I'm blanking on at the moment told their stories. <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T07:38:00-07:00","id":1516776,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516771#post1516771\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I will mention that, as far as Wraith goes, one of the chapters in the Book of Haunts was about a Scottish castle.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this was Glamis or Glams Castle, if memory serves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T07:45:05-07:00","id":1516778,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516776#post1516776\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think this was Glamis or Glams Castle, if memory serves.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it was <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermitage_Castle\" target=\"_blank\">Hermitage Castle</a>, IIRC. I can't recall if Mary, Queen of Scots was one of the characters in it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T09:15:14-07:00","id":1516798,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">That\u2019s a lot of answers thank you! I\u2019ll try to go through it all. It's a bit all over the place so I'll try to keep it in order :</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Places and a bit of spirits:</b> Regarding the lochs they definitely play an important role in the story since it\u2019s so specific to the country. I initially thought of caves as \u2018natural\u2019 entrance to the underworld, but lochs could be one as well that\u2019s a very interesting idea. Or they could be entrances to \u2018something\u2019. That would go with many stories of people disappearing around some of them (often ending up as an abducting by fairy story) in addition to the fairy trods all around the place. They actually use an example of fairy trod in scotland in Umbra W20.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">There are also the Each Uisge, a water horse that\u2019s a lot more nefarious than the medium kelpie as they are carnivorous and love eating humans. It is said that they guard the watery path to the afterlife where our ancestors live on so that could relate. I\u2019m not yet totally sold on whether they are a different spirit or corrupted kelpies since they took a very big hit after the howler fells.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I also wanted to use more the ley lines but haven\u2019t looked too much into it. I didn\u2019t think about simply checking a map\u2026 I definitely need to check how many goes through the highlands, but it could definitely be places where things go crazy. As places of interest, it would indeed add quite some pressure from other surnat using places of power and help keep the \u2018werewolves of Scotland are under permanent duress\u2019 thing going on even in remote areas. That\u2019s where I need to step up my fae/mage game.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Spirits: </b>There are a lot of \u2018water monster\u2019 stories going around everywhere -almost every loch has one- and I think of them as \u2018great beasts\u2019, when it\u2019s not water horses, similar to the ones you can summon with the rank 5 gift. I\u2019m want to use the \u2018pictish beast\u2019 as one of those that was in Lion\u2019s brood at some point as well. Contrarily to Green dragon she didn\u2019t follow the howler in their fall, but became an agent of chaos and havoc everywhere she would go.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I use water horses (kelpie specifically) as a trickster spirits of seduction and water. They are a mix of the spirit presented in the white howler book and axis mundis.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The will-\u2018o-wisp as a spirits that touch on other world is interesting. I\u2019m usually a bit wary of things that are too crossover as it would make werewolf be naturally a lot more aware of everything else, with a lot more knowledge. Thought I already imagine it as trickster spirits that play at losing people in \u2018open way\u2019 to otherworldly places just to see what happens. Since my Scottish setting have a potentially many places where crossing would be quite doable even for a human I see a nice synergy that could fuel fey or ghosts story alike.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I\u2019ve created an offspring of Owl, lemur-owl (that sounds a lot better in French), an aspect of Owl only interested about death/ghosts/dark umbra knowledge and wisdom that was part of Lion\u2019s brood in the old days. I use the Shadow-ish black/green dogs (especially the Cu-sith) as messenger/herald of death in lemur-owl brood.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The idea is that they roam the land and would originally be benevolent spirits that appears/bark to people when they are about to step where they shouldn\u2019t, like a place mixed up with the shadowlands. This would often result in a sad ending, sometimes not, leaving them to be falsely accused of causing death and be nefarious spirits, which slowly made them evolve toward what the stories tell about them (that\u2019s where you get barghests and other type of black dogs).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Fomori:</b> I haven\u2019t thought at all about fomori families, but that\u2019s indeed something interesting I could keep in mind. In addition I play a lot around the BSD-Howler link. Having some old fomori families that could be a bit like the cluithi camp of BSD and able to trace back their lineage to the romans and/or Howler kin.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I have to double check on the pretanic order too because it\u2019s been a while, but I remember them as a cult of humans/fomori able to open the way to malfeas and al the joyful things that can happen after that. I could link them to the idea of very old fomori family, but I have to check f it can be a match.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Vampire:</b> I imagine the south as the most \u2018weaver\u2019 touched part of the country, with of course any city of decent size. I also thought that it would be where vampire concentrate mostly, I think I\u2019m going to keep the canon, but I don\u2019t think my players are going to see much of vampire but thank you for the info because it give me at least a very wide idea of the Scottish canvas for them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I have a particular interest about the Dunsirn (thanks for the link) as I can make them descendant of howler then Fianna stock that have gone wrong and ended up in BSD hands. I like to keep some elements tight between the howler and the bsd so when my player digs their tribe past and what they have left behind it\u2019s not always a pleasant surprise. It\u2019s another perfect opportunity here.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">There\u2019s some vampire scattered in the wilderness yeah, that has to be considered. But hey would probably be the ones that the pcs wouldn\u2019t meet since they must be very good at avoiding the werewolves to survive in the wild. So yeah, they exist, but probably the ones that won\u2019t be seen unless there is a very specific reason.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I didn\u2019t know the Cailleach was actually used in vampire but after Baba yaga I guess it\u2019s no surprise. I was going to use her as a fae or spirit but I will consider the vampire option, it could be quite unexpected for my pcs and a way to bring vampires on the table. I\u2019ve considered Lhiannan since the old paganism is a recurrent topic (I actually created some very old howler gifts with inspiration from ogham) but I don\u2019t want to steal the light from my pcs (one have a concept around this idea) so I\u2019ll probably turn my gaze on Gangrel or nosferatu.<br /> And yeah I agree too if I want to go on the Cailleach/vampire hag route it could be the line of UK Gangrels that hunted the Lhiannan down and ate them, making them inheritors of powers similar to Koldunic Sorcery (I want to keep it simple most of my \u2018heavy\u2019 work goes on werewolf).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Wraith:</b> The howler had this link with the restless dead, so I consider BSD to have kept some of that heritage to a certain degree and actually some of their gift use ghosts. They would have only some very specific knowledge related to their needs and how they use ghosts, but it\u2019s another topic where it crosses with the White howlers.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Yeah, werewolves don\u2019t usually become wraith, I would probably give it as much chances as to become an abomination. So not a lot, but still a bit since a lot more werewolves are dying before their time in comparison to the number of embraces. But for all the reason I mentioned I also consider it could happen more in Scotland.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The known howler npc that became a ghost is Cororuc, supposedly the last of his kind that had time to run to a Fianna sept, tell them what happened, died, couldn\u2019t become an ancestor spirit, and became a ghost instead. I don\u2019t know if I will go all the way to a penance arc with him, but I leave the possibility for him to at least be able to open up to the pcs and be one of their bridges in the dark umbra. He would be an old ghost and powerful ally that would teach them many things from history and culture of their tribe to dark umbra knowledge and gift/rites related to it.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Good to know that a Scottish place was presented for Wraith, I\u2019ll have to take a look and add it on my map then!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Fae/Changelin:</b> I\u2019m not going to lie I think a bit of the same regarding changeling and my player don\u2019t expect much from it. I like the whole fae setting with the courts (seelie/unseelie, or the season ones for the UK if I\u2019m not mistaken) and I think I going to stick to that, just keeping the idea that with everything that took the glamour away, they fell from \u2018gods\u2019 to \u2018sad, bitter and weak\u2019 though for me the weak have to be thought about in Scotland, depending where you are. There are fae roads everywhere, old fae domain and such and I have already prepare something where a very old and -used to be- powerful fae stayed behind because of an oath.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Thank you for the blog I will have a look into it! But yeah for that one the idea is to be able to work on the absolute tons of legends and folks stories related to the fairies. I\u2019ll take a look into those Terror tales series, it can at least be a good source for ideas.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Mages :</b> Thank you for the detail related to mage, I\u2019ve played a bit of ars magica (a merinita) so the whole house diedne/Tremere thing talks to me. So basically I can go to more or less what I want for mages, for the tradition ones at least. Ofc I had this big clich\u00e9 with verbena but that was what I wanted to avoid. I have some bits and pieces of info related to mages through other games, but it's either middle ages info, or second hands (like in the supplement for changeling in the british isles)</span><br /> <br /> <b>Hunter:</b><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"> To be fair I know next to nothing about that one, just that they are some humans with powers that can relate to 'true faith'. I think the most I read was with inquisitor/wich hunter in vampire supplement like British isles and those are probably long gone. I\u2019ll add Hunter: the reckoning, The Wayward creed and the Moonstruck in my to check list! </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I forgot a bit the 'humans' but that's true I wouldn't imagine it without the government or at least a governmental entity that has its nose, or at least its eyes, on the whole supernat sheningans going on. That and very likely several cults going on in addition to the 'old ways' still followed in the highlands</span><br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8944-tanakyelle","name":"Tanakyelle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8944_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T09:49:47-07:00","id":1516802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516778#post1516778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it was <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermitage_Castle\" target=\"_blank\">Hermitage Castle</a>, IIRC. I can't recall if Mary, Queen of Scots was one of the characters in it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah I couldn\u2019t remember, mind you Mary Queen of Scots would be an interesting wraith, especially with Henry Tudor being the regent of the Tower of London and his imprisoning Elizabeth 1st (but I digress)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T11:52:09-07:00","id":1516812,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516778#post1516778\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it was <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermitage_Castle\" target=\"_blank\">Hermitage Castle</a>, IIRC. I can't recall if Mary, Queen of Scots was one of the characters in it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, it was Hermitage Castle in the Haunts sourcebook from 1994. It is a stronghold of the Hierarchy, and in fact is home to the Anacreons of the Legions, which perhaps implies the Hierarchy of Edinburgh region is ruled from there.<br /> <br /> Like most Wraith products, the Haunts sourcebook is extremely well done. It provides a lot of examples of how to design Haunts of many different types and importance for the ST. I think Richard Dansky did an excellent job as Developer, but I have a special place in my heart for the books done under Wraith's first developer, Jennifer Hartshorn.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-30T13:50:03-07:00","id":1516817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516798#post1516798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I\u2019ve created an offspring of Owl, lemur-owl (that sounds a lot better in French), an aspect of Owl only interested about death/ghosts/dark umbra knowledge and wisdom that was part of Lion\u2019s brood in the old days. I use the Shadow-ish black/green dogs (especially the Cu-sith) as messenger/herald of death in lemur-owl brood.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>We have a breed of Owl Shifters in our campaign the Uwila (taken from the Olde English for Owl), very secretive, unknown to the Garou and other Fera, surviving the War of Rage through their secrecy and arcane lore.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516798#post1516798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[FONT=Calibri]<b>Wraith:</b><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Yeah, werewolves don\u2019t usually become wraith, I would probably give it as much chances as to become an abomination. So not a lot, but still a bit since a lot more werewolves are dying before their time in comparison to the number of embraces. But for all the reason I mentioned I also consider it could happen more in Scotland.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>Personally I think they\u2019d be more common than Abominations, as there are Croatian Wraiths as well.<br /> Not immensely common but I can see tribes or lodges that frequent the Dark Umbra being prone to becoming Wraiths, or those that traffic a lot with Death Spirits or Owls brood.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516798#post1516798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Fae/Changelin:</b> I\u2019m not going to lie I think a bit of the same regarding changeling and my player don\u2019t expect much from it. I like the whole fae setting with the courts (seelie/unseelie, or the season ones for the UK if I\u2019m not mistaken) and I think I going to stick to that, just keeping the idea that with everything that took the glamour away, they fell from \u2018gods\u2019 to \u2018sad, bitter and weak\u2019 though for me the weak have to be thought about in Scotland, depending where you are. There are fae roads everywhere, old fae domain and such and I have already prepare something where a very old and -used to be- powerful fae stayed behind because of an oath.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>To be honest I understand why people don\u2019t like Changeling, but I do like it albeit we include it with a very heavy degree of Homebrew/house rules.<br /> <br /> The biggest issue with Changeling is it\u2019s incompatibility with the other splats and contradicting existing links, the Fey are portrayed as fickle jesters to the Fianna despite millennia of alliances and oathes (same with the Nunnehi and Uktena).<br /> <br /> We also include elements of CTL such as the Hedge etc to make the fey a lot darker.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516798#post1516798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Mages :</b> Thank you for the detail related to mage, I\u2019ve played a bit of ars magica (a merinita) so the whole house diedne/Tremere thing talks to me. So basically I can go to more or less what I want for mages, for the tradition ones at least. Ofc I had this big clich\u00e9 with verbena but that was what I wanted to avoid. I have some bits and pieces of info related to mages through other games, but it's either middle ages info, or second hands (like in the supplement for changeling in the british isles)</span>\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I\u2019d really recommend looking at the Mage Thread: <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1504795-homebrew-headcanons-pt-2-mystical-boogaloo\">https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/...tical-boogaloo</a>\u200b, even if you don\u2019t use Mage it gives a lot of ideas for Druidic chapters of traditions such as the Order of Hermes etc as an alternative to the Verbena.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-01T05:12:29-07:00","id":1516882,"title":"","content":"The Loch Ness Monster always sounded like a Bygone Beast or more like a Mokol\u00e9 (or a plesiod Dragon King who refused to evolve/devolve in to a mokol\u00e9)<br /> The Kelpies, supernatural water horse hunts Scotland's lochs and lonely rivers? Scary Specters<br /> Selkies are no brainer<br /> Fingal's Cave, a cave is formed entirely from hexagonally jointed basalt columns? Could be the oldest Weaver &quot;Caern&quot;, before her madness, reflecing Nature symmetry, logic and order - the last bastion of a &quot;Sane&quot; weaver avatar<br /> While the Corryvreckan whirlpool is a Wyld-nexus<br /> <br /> The Orcadian folklore is rich with ideas, Mester Stoor Worm, the gigantic evil sea serpent and the nuckelavee, a horse-like demon from Orcadian folklore that combines equine and human elements are one of the most terrifying myths<br /> <br /> The Ghost Pier of Clanyard Bay speaks for itself<br /> The C\u00f9-s\u00ecth, the Banshee, the Big Grey Man of Ben Macdhui, the Blue Men of the Minch, the hybrid creature of a goat and a manm, the Urisk, the Fachan, the Glaistig, the Green Maiden... you can't toss a stone around scottland without hiting something supernatural, really<br /> Truly, if anywhere the Dreaming is strong and gates to Arcadia exist, that will be there<br /> <br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T07:29:08-07:00","id":1516969,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516882#post1516882\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Fingal's Cave, a cave is formed entirely from hexagonally jointed basalt columns? Could be the oldest Weaver &quot;Caern&quot;, before her madness, reflecing Nature symmetry, logic and order - the last bastion of a &quot;Sane&quot; weaver avatar</div> </div> </div> </div>Also The Giants Causeway MacCool and the Scottish giant Benandonner\u200b, according to legend the Causeway stretched from Ireland to Scotland and might still in the dreaming.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516882#post1516882\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The C\u00f9-s\u00ecth, the Banshee, the Big Grey Man of Ben Macdhui, the Blue Men of the Minch, the hybrid creature of a goat and a manm, the Urisk, the Fachan, the Glaistig, the Green Maiden... you can't toss a stone around scottland without hiting something supernatural, really<br /> Truly, if anywhere the Dreaming is strong and gates to Arcadia exist, that will be there</div> </div> </div> </div>This is how I see Scotland as being one of the larger Fey strongholds with a wild chimeric landscape and also a haunted land with an ancient shadowland and very old ghosts.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-02T14:04:14-07:00","id":1516997,"title":"","content":"Speaking of Loch Ness, here\u2019s some interesting tid bits I think are cool and could be useful for a variety of settings.<br /> <br /> \u2022Aleister Crowley (yes, that Crowley; The Great Beast 666, Most Evil Man Alive, Prophet of the Aeon of Horus, etc.) had a manor on Loch Ness (Boleskine Manor; later owned by Jimmy Page and burning down in 2015) where Crowley as a young man still tied with the Golden Dawn supposedly carried out an incomplete Magickal Working. Apparently some occultists think that Crowley\u2019s incomplete working might have kicked up some occult juices around Loch Ness which made the appearances of the Loch Ness Monster more significant.<br /> <br /> Similarly there\u2019s also an anecdote where Crowley claims to have seen a water nymph while climbing up a waterfall-rock face at Loch Ness.<br /> <br /> Related with that field of odd tid bits about Fun Fact and speculation from various esoteric groups, there are some claims that the Lock Ness Monster is not a cryptic or living fossil but actually some sort of spiritual entity related to a pre-Christian Dragon-Serpent Cult (perhaps one brought to Britain by the Vikings), veneration of which may have been preserved in folk rituals up through the Industrial Revolution (where supposedly villagers and peasants would toss the first batch of beer every year into the water as an offering in hopes of good harvests and bountiful sea produce such as fish, sea glass, drift wood, no drownings, etc.<br /> <br /> Any number of the above could be used as interesting plot hooks I think and could be used as inspiration for any region, not just Loch Ness imo.<br /> <br /> I can easily see the Crowley story as either a Mage or Werewolf plot hook where a prodigy occultist leaves a ritual undone and the players might come across summoned entities that have been running loose ever since.<br /> <br /> One could have the idea of a pre-Christian Serpent cult be tied either to the Fianna, Get of Fenris, or even Black Spiral Dancers kinfolk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-03T02:23:50-07:00","id":1517046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516997#post1516997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speaking of Loch Ness, here\u2019s some interesting tid bits I think are cool and could be useful, Crowley as a young man still tied with the Golden Dawn supposedly carried out an incomplete Magickal Working. Apparently some occultists think that Crowley\u2019s incomplete working might have kicked up some occult juices around Loch Ness which made the appearances of the Loch Ness Monster more significant.<br /> <br /> Similarly there\u2019s also an anecdote where Crowley claims to have seen a water nymph while climbing up a waterfall-rock face at Loch Ness.<br /> <br /> I can easily see the Crowley story as either a Mage or Werewolf plot hook where a prodigy occultist leaves a ritual undone and the players might come across summoned entities that have been running loose ever since.</div> </div> </div> </div>Think Crowley\u2019s one of those interesting figures that was done a bit of a disservice by being another historical person just embraced.<br /> <br /> I like <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/\">Ragged Robin</a>\u2019s idea of foundations non-supernatural people who gain huge levels of inertia and coincidental protection, but I do also like the idea of his being an irresponsible sorceror or orphan Mage recklessly failing to complete a ritual.<br /> <br /> I do think he had high levels of awareness or even mystical/Fey blood which could explain his seeing umbral entities or Fey creatures.<br /> <br /> Or the Mists are less effective near the Lochs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516997#post1516997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One could have the idea of a pre-Christian Serpent cult be tied either to the Fianna, Get of Fenris, or even Black Spiral Dancers kinfolk.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nesse could be one of the Great Beasts or as others have said a bygone, or Great Worm.<br /> <br /> The concept of Worm cults has great potential, considering the number of worm legends around Britain (albeit many are dragons or told as such), as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/\">Shadeprowler</a> suggested it could be another manifestation of the Mester Stoor Worm.<br /> <br /> Perhaps Crowleys broken ritual exploited the natural gateways of the Loch (to the Penumbra, Shadowlands, Near Dreaming) and opened an Anchorhead into the Deep Umbra.<br /> <br /> Personally I like to link Worm Cults with Crom Cruach and outer beings, so it could be an interesting antagonist for Garou, Mage or others and maybe something that the BSDs or black magicians.<br /> <br /> Alternatively perhaps it a Wyld creature and the BSDs want to capture or sacrifice it, maybe the old harvest rituals have a dark undertone and Crowley\u2019s research touched upon a fragmented account of summoning a spirit of magic to be sacrificed.<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-04T23:47:39-07:00","id":1517198,"title":"","content":"I would probably just make the Loch Ness Monster one of the &quot;Great Beasts&quot; of Garou legend (summonable by the Gift: Song of the Great Beast). As one of (many) Lake Serpents, it even has stats in the first edition Werewolf Storytellers Handbook.<br /> <br /> As such, I would probably make Loch Ness sacred to the local Garou, and perhaps even a site of pilgrimage by whatever lupus remain on the British Isles.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-05T00:11:49-07:00","id":1517199,"title":"","content":"Good idea <b><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\">Black Fox</a></b>\u200b<br /> Alas the Garou tend to associate everything that have scales and/or serpentine/draconic body with the Wyrm<br /> The Mokol\u00e9 even lamented about this and the fact, the Garou named the <i>Dissolver</i>\u200b the &quot;Wyrm&quot; as the part of a slender campagin to drag the Mokol\u00e9 &quot;reputation&quot; in to the mud and justify their crimes against them<br /> In the &quot;birthplace of the Black Spiral Dancers&quot;, Scotland (and the place of the Garou gratest shame where they not helped the White Howlers) I doubt they would see Nessie as a minion of Gaia and her home a scared place - more like an avatar/spawn of the Green Dragon<br /> But that is just my two cents <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-11T07:45:26-07:00","id":1517700,"title":"","content":"Sorry for the long delay !<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516817#post1516817\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I think they\u2019d be more common than Abominations, as there are Croatian Wraiths as well.<br /> Not immensely common but I can see tribes or lodges that frequent the Dark Umbra being prone to becoming Wraiths, or those that traffic a lot with Death Spirits or Owls brood.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are sort-of Bunyips ghost-ish spirits as well. I haven't dug too much in the topic and I remember those being spirits, but definitely acting like angry ghosts or at least that's what I remember from my read of it, the vocabulary being very focus around angry, vengeance haunting and such themes. But yeah I also think that there are more garou-ghosts than abomination simply because of the amount of werewolf that die brutal deaths, rather than be embraced.<br /> <br /> <br /> The fingal cave can be used in many way. My take on it is that the giant's causeway and fingal's cave are two piece of an originally unique very powerful fae font of power. Through exchange of oath the south was exploited by the Fianna and the north by the fae. The bridge between the two doesn't exist anymore on earth but is still there in the umbra if you know how to use it. I know that fae/changelin have this trod things but don't know the specifics so I plan to make it a &quot;fast travel&quot; thing with a fae taste to it ans since I enjoy the whole &quot;music&quot; theme around fingal's cave I'll probably make it something like a specific tune you have to play.<br /> There could definitely still be the giant from the story still around.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516969#post1516969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is how I see Scotland as being one of the larger Fey strongholds with a wild chimeric landscape and also a haunted land with an ancient shadowland and very old ghosts.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly, I see scotland (and Ireland by extention) a bit like Egypt or the asian countries in the wod. A place were the supernatural is, and always has been very present and &quot;seen&quot;. People are more aware of it even if they don't talk about it, either because they simply know, or because they know that not follow customs can be a very expensive mistake.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1516997#post1516997\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Speaking of Loch Ness, here\u2019s some interesting tid bits I think are cool and could be useful for a variety of settings.<br /> <br /> \u2022Aleister Crowley (yes, that Crowley; The Great Beast 666, Most Evil Man Alive, Prophet of the Aeon of Horus, etc.) had a manor on Loch Ness (Boleskine Manor; later owned by Jimmy Page and burning down in 2015) where Crowley as a young man still tied with the Golden Dawn supposedly carried out an incomplete Magickal Working. Apparently some occultists think that Crowley\u2019s incomplete working might have kicked up some occult juices around Loch Ness which made the appearances of the Loch Ness Monster more significant.<br /> <br /> Similarly there\u2019s also an anecdote where Crowley claims to have seen a water nymph while climbing up a waterfall-rock face at Loch Ness.<br /> <br /> Related with that field of odd tid bits about Fun Fact and speculation from various esoteric groups, there are some claims that the Lock Ness Monster is not a cryptic or living fossil but actually some sort of spiritual entity related to a pre-Christian Dragon-Serpent Cult (perhaps one brought to Britain by the Vikings), veneration of which may have been preserved in folk rituals up through the Industrial Revolution (where supposedly villagers and peasants would toss the first batch of beer every year into the water as an offering in hopes of good harvests and bountiful sea produce such as fish, sea glass, drift wood, no drownings, etc.<br /> <br /> Any number of the above could be used as interesting plot hooks I think and could be used as inspiration for any region, not just Loch Ness imo.<br /> <br /> I can easily see the Crowley story as either a Mage or Werewolf plot hook where a prodigy occultist leaves a ritual undone and the players might come across summoned entities that have been running loose ever since.<br /> <br /> One could have the idea of a pre-Christian Serpent cult be tied either to the Fianna, Get of Fenris, or even Black Spiral Dancers kinfolk.</div> </div> </div> </div>The ritual thing is very funny and I if I'm not mistaken it's the exact reason why the egyptian umbra is so different : Mages played with it and shit happened, it made the gauntlet so thin that anything can cross without effort. You could actually have a family of sorcerers, or even just humans waiting for the next &quot;chosen&quot; to be born in order to close it. They could have old family stories and legend related to the work of the ancestor that did this.<br /> <br /> The pre-christian cult of the dragon is a good idea can definitely be a thing easily introduced without having it tied to loch ness there are many potentially reptile creatures (or not reptile even) : Any Lake/sea serpent like the ones inhabiting the lochs, the Stoorworm, Beinn a' Bheithir... I don't know it very well because I'm playing it rn, but I believe the game Nephilim have a full campaign describing a cult worshipping a dragon-like creature (Drakaon) that can probably be easily exported to flesh it out.\u200b<br /> <br /> The loch ness monster is definitely a great beast for me, like most lake serpent.<br /> In werewolf DA they say that Fiannas holds a fest every summer there it's not hard to imagine that it's a place of importance for them. While it's true werewolves tend to see reptile creatures as wyrm associates it's not always the case, you even have totems that are reptiles and not of the wyrm. For example the fenris have two dragons presented in their tribebook. I think it's an interesting topic though and you can have an infinite reasons/stories related to this. The werewolf could have vanquished the creatures many years ago but they keep the legend alive because &quot;reasons&quot;. Could be an oath with fairies (I think this kind of things fuel the glamour/dreaming ?) or more simply to boast of what their ancestor did, tell their legends and so on. The evening of the fest is a contest for the best story related to the loch ness monster so you can more or less make it whatever you want it to be.<br /> <br /> Another of those lake monster officially presented is indeed associated to the wyrm since it's the guardian of the mile deep loch caern, known as Cirig the Creast-Headed for the Black spiral dancers and Ard Cruimh Beitioch for the Fianna and fenris. The few bits and piece i read about it let me think it's a sort of being similar to a zmei/great beast as it was a colossal creature and a massive fight that brought it down. They didn't manage to kill it and it has been sealed. There's another one in Rite of passage, this one described as lamprey-like with a similar stat spread that the one presented in the Storyteller handbook.<br /> <br /> My take on the reptile-like creatures/spirits of scotland is that I separate them in two broad types, the aquatic ones and the terrestrial ones. The water ones would be related to the pictish beast (more or less the mother of lake/sea monsters / queen of the fuath) and the terrestrials ones to Green dragon that would have fall to the wyrm with their spiritual patron. The lake serpent were great beast invoked by white howler long ago, and while a some of the brood fell with them not all did, and those that didn't got very angry at the betrayal and are usually hostiles to werewolves keeping the narrative of reptile creatures = bad shit wery much alive.<br /> --&gt; This goes well in my game as it's one among several things that makes the white howler tribe a tribe with dangerous/tendentious topics/skills/allies. It's up to the players to see if they want to keep the heritage of their tribe &quot;as it was&quot; or if they will let a lot of it behind them, it's interesting to see their choices.<br /> <br /> Funny bit : regarding the Fianna shame it's very tame in my game. It's a thing to sing about it to draw out emotion but it's en entire other business to go see the reborn Howlers in the eye and tell them &quot;we are sorry&quot;. While the Fianna are doing a lot of sacrifices for the new Howler, the shame-thing have been tuned down a lot. Let me tell you that it makes my players very bitter !","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8944-tanakyelle","name":"Tanakyelle","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8944_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-11T12:37:25-07:00","id":1517708,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1517700#post1517700\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Funny bit : regarding the Fianna shame it's very tame in my game. It's a thing to sing about it to draw out emotion but it's en entire other business to go see the reborn Howlers in the eye and tell them &quot;we are sorry&quot;. While the Fianna are doing a lot of sacrifices for the new Howler, the shame-thing have been tuned down a lot. Let me tell you that it makes my players very bitter !</div> </div> </div> </div>The fall of the White Howlers is something that has dramatically changed a lot through the editions. There have been major changes which impact the setting severely.<br /> <br /> In first edition Book of the Wyrm, the White Howlers themselves were superb specimens of Garou - the only ones who could truly defeat the legions of Rome (and the fomori who came with them) despite their relatively small numbers. As a result, the Wyrm tried a different approach. Somebody - perhaps a fomori, a Bane, or some other Wyrm minion - activated a secret Pit in Scotland that summoned Banes which quickly possessed the White Howler kinfolk and deformed them. One pack went into the Pit to destroy it, but they failed and became the first Black Spiral Dancers. This single pack them came out, killed many of the White Howlers (presumably by ambush and surprise) and forced the others to walk the spiral. Cororuc (note the spelling) was simply the only member of the first pack to enter the Spiral to not be corrupted, and the others killed him before they ambushed the other Howlers. So in this version of events, there is definitely no &quot;shame&quot; for any other Garou. Nobody could have expected what was going to happen. And a strong argument could be said that even the White Howlers are not really to blame for what happened given the speed by which it happened. (And Cororuc - instead of being the last White Howler - was actually the first one killed by the new Black Spiral Dancers.)<br /> <br /> This was heavily based on Robert E. Howard's stories of Bran Mak Morn. The Howlers themselves mirrored the physical perfection of Bran while the corrupted kinfolk mirrored the degenerate status of the Picts themselves.<br /> <br /> In second edition Book of the Wyrm, the corruption and decline of the White Howler kinfolk, instead of happening relatively quickly as a result of some nefarious Bane activity, occurred over a longer period of time. The White Howlers themselves allowed their Rage to control their behavior too much. And this was possibly the reason the tribe routinely descended into Malfeas to test their worth. The Howlers themselves choose to make dark pacts with infernal forces to stop the Roman advance into Scotland. These corrupted Howlers waged war against the uncorrupted Howlers, and they killed Coruroc (note the spelling change) as the last uncorrupted White Howler. Presumably this was when the remaining, corrupted, Howlers walked the Black Spiral and became the Dancers.<br /> <br /> A different version was given in the 2e Players Guide. In this version, the states the bulk of the tribe, filled with pride and hubris, went down a Wyrmhole and came out as Black Spiral Dancers. It was only then that the Dancers corrupted their kinfolk!<br /> <br /> In these versions, again the rest of the Garou have no reason to have any shame. Except now the White Howlers themselves are responsible for their own demise. So this is very different than what happened in 1e.<br /> <br /> I don't think there was ever a version for Revised. It just kept the 2e story (or stories since there are two versions of them).<br /> <br /> The W20 corebook version is different still. There's no more talk of degenerated kinfolk. Instead, the White Howlers decide en masse as a tribe they are going to fight the Wyrm in its lair in Malfeas and achieve great glory. Instead, they end up walking the Labyrinth and all become Black Spiral Dancers at once. Only Cororuc escaped and managed to inform a Fianna sept before he died. Unlike 2e, the White Howlers are not responsible for their own corruption. It was just a tactical attack against the Wyrm that went horribly wrong.<br /> <br /> There is yet a FIFTH version in the W20 White Howler tribebook gives us a variant of the W20 corebook story. This time we're told that the Howlers had to deal with Rome and an outbreak of Wyrm activity at the same time. It looked like the Romans were going to conquer Caledonia. So the tribe gathered and debated what to do. They decided the entire White Howler tribe would travel south and attack the Roman fortresses and destroy the Roman legions and any fomori and Wyrm creatures also there. This took months to do (despite that by using Moon Bridges they need only take a few days at most to put themselves into position), and when they finally attacked they didn't find many legions or Wyrm minions.<br /> <br /> When the Howlers came back bloodied (presumably by butchering thousands of defenseless civilian humans), but victorious they found that while they were gone all the Roman legions and fomori had devastated their homeland and their kinfolk had become corrupted. (At least that missing detail from the corebook is now filled.) Many had become fomori and the Howlers spent years destroying them as well as sealing off the Pits or Wyrmholes the Roman fomori had created. But still the land and kinfolk remain corrupted. But eventually they made their way to a great Pit in northwestern Scotland which they found lead to Malfeas itself. (I should note that in second edition Chronicle of the Black Labyrinth, it's clear that the passage that leads to Malfeas is in southeast Scotland somewhere near the county of Fife so the geography does not even match.)<br /> <br /> This lead to a big discussion among the White Howlers on what to do, and they eventually decided to invite all the tribes to help them invade Malfeas. The other tribes didn't respond, so the Howlers cursed them and then went in alone and became the Black Spiral Dancers.<br /> \u200b<br /> It is this fifth version, and ONLY the fifth version, that lays out any blame to the other Garou tribes. For me, the W20 White Howler tribebook gives the worst origin of the Black Spiral Dancers. It doesn't make sense on several levels. It departs the most from actual history. It has several plot holes. It gets the location of the first Pit wrong. And it is the only one that smears the rest of the Garou Nation. Blech.<br /> <br /> I personally like the first edition version the best. The second edition has some merit, but by making the White Howlers corrupt BEFORE they entered the Labyrinth, it robs the significance of the Black Spiral itself. The W20 version just smears the Garou as a whole. The latter editions really wanted to make the Garou their own enemy. Which is OK to a limited degree, but too over played.<br /> <br /> The major flaw of the 1e version is that it is incomplete, and the ST needs to fill in a lot of details. There's a lot of vagueness of the Wyrm did this and the Wyrm did that. It would be much better if we know who exactly activated the Pit that corrupted the kinfolk, what kind of fomori they have transformed into, which kinfolk might have been &quot;saved&quot; but later tainted the Fianna to some degree, how Whippoorwill (or more likely Nightjar since Nightjar is native to Scotland and the Whippoorwill isn't) became the tribal totem, how this single Dancer pack killed all the other Howlers, and other such details. So we can pillage some bits of the other editions to improve it. But I think fundamentally it is the best version of the five we've been given.<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-11T14:05:33-07:00","id":1517714,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1517700#post1517700\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The fingal cave can be used in many way. My take on it is that the giant's causeway and fingal's cave are two piece of an originally unique very powerful fae font of power. Through exchange of oath the south was exploited by the Fianna and the north by the fae. The bridge between the two doesn't exist anymore on earth but is still there in the umbra if you know how to use it. I know that fae/changelin have this trod things but don't know the specifics so I plan to make it a &quot;fast travel&quot; thing with a fae taste to it ans since I enjoy the whole &quot;music&quot; theme around fingal's cave I'll probably make it something like a specific tune you have to play.<br /> There could definitely still be the giant from the story still around.</div> </div> </div> </div>That works well, I like the idea of there being hidden or mythic places that can be accessed through rituals.<br /> Changeling has the Dreaming so the music could summon the path from there, folklore has has Fairy Paths straight lines between Raths or fairy forts, perhaps there\u2019s a Rath on both the Irish and Scottish coasts with a giant guardian.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1517700#post1517700\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Exactly, I see scotland (and Ireland by extention) a bit like Egypt or the asian countries in the wod. A place were the supernatural is, and always has been very present and &quot;seen&quot;. People are more aware of it even if they don't talk about it, either because they simply know, or because they know that not follow customs can be a very expensive mistake.</div> </div> </div> </div>The scene from the Slaughtered Lamb Pub from The American Werewolf in London.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tanakyelle</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1517700#post1517700\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The pre-christian cult of the dragon is a good idea can definitely be a thing easily introduced without having it tied to loch ness there are many potentially reptile creatures (or not reptile even) : Any Lake/sea serpent like the ones inhabiting the lochs, the Stoorworm, Beinn a' Bheithir... I don't know it very well because I'm playing it rn, but I believe the game Nep