 to the pictish beast (more or less the mother of lake/sea monsters / queen of the fuath) and the terrestrials ones to Green dragon that would have fall to the wyrm with their spiritual patron. The lake serpent were great beast invoked by white howler long ago, and while a some of the brood fell with them not all did, and those that didn't got very angry at the betrayal and are usually hostiles to werewolves keeping the narrative of reptile creatures = bad shit wery much alive.<br /> --&gt; This goes well in my game as it's one among several things that makes the white howler tribe a tribe with dangerous/tendentious topics/skills/allies. It's up to the players to see if they want to keep the heritage of their tribe &quot;as it was&quot; or if they will let a lot of it behind them, it's interesting to see their choices.</div> </div> </div> </div>Theres lots of ideas here, I tend to think that the Baga Yaga knew some powerful Wyrm rites that consecrated the Zmei to one of the Urge Wyrms protecting them from Fading (Paradox), other terrestrial mythic/bygones could have been blessed by the Wyrm affording them the same protection.<br /> <br /> The Americas and Russian aren\u2019t the only places where there were titanic banes or Wyrm monsters such as the Zmei, creatures to powerful to be killed but can be bound, the Howlers could have fulfilled a role in the Celtic world similar to the Uktena in keeping dragons, serpents and worms bound.<br /> <br /> Its possible that the Great Serpent Nesse is a guardian that patrols the waterways of the umbra scarring off or eating anything that tries to get close to Cirigs prison.<br /> <br /> The Finana may hold their festival every summer picking up the sacred duty from the Howlers, particularly from the Howler kinfolk they managed to save, to renew the great beasts gnosis and renewing their bonds.<br /> Of course the BSDs would like nothing more that disrupting the festival, driving Nesse into the Deeper Umbra and freeing Cirig.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-11T15:07:23-07:00","id":1517715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1517708#post1517708\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The fall of the White Howlers is something that has dramatically changed a lot through the editions. There have been major changes which impact the setting severely.<br /> <br /> In first edition Book of the Wyrm, the White Howlers themselves were superb specimens of Garou - the only ones who could truly defeat the legions of Rome (and the fomori who came with them) despite their relatively small numbers. As a result, the Wyrm tried a different approach. Somebody - perhaps a fomori, a Bane, or some other Wyrm minion - activated a secret Pit in Scotland that summoned Banes which quickly possessed the White Howler kinfolk and deformed them. One pack went into the Pit to destroy it, but they failed and became the first Black Spiral Dancers. This single pack them came out, killed many of the White Howlers (presumably by ambush and surprise) and forced the others to walk the spiral. Cororuc (note the spelling) was simply the only member of the first pack to enter the Spiral to not be corrupted, and the others killed him before they ambushed the other Howlers. So in this version of events, there is definitely no &quot;shame&quot; for any other Garou. Nobody could have expected what was going to happen. And a strong argument could be said that even the White Howlers are not really to blame for what happened given the speed by which it happened. (And Cororuc - instead of being the last White Howler - was actually the first one killed by the new Black Spiral Dancers.)<br /> <br /> This was heavily based on Robert E. Howard's stories of Bran Mak Morn. The Howlers themselves mirrored the physical perfection of Bran while the corrupted kinfolk mirrored the degenerate status of the Picts themselves.<br /> <br /> The major flaw of the 1e version is that it is incomplete, and the ST needs to fill in a lot of details. There's a lot of vagueness of the Wyrm did this and the Wyrm did that. It would be much better if we know who exactly activated the Pit that corrupted the kinfolk, what kind of fomori they have transformed into, which kinfolk might have been &quot;saved&quot; but later tainted the Fianna to some degree, how Whippoorwill (or more likely Nightjar since Nightjar is native to Scotland and the Whippoorwill isn't) became the tribal totem, how this single Dancer pack killed all the other Howlers, and other such details. So we can pillage some bits of the other editions to improve it. But I think fundamentally it is the best version of the five we've been given.<br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree there\u2019s enough Garou shame with the War of Rage, War of Shame and so on, the fall of the Howlers fits best as tragic sacrifice.<br /> <br /> My headcanon would be that the pack discovered an anchorhead to Malfeas, they were deceived drawn into attacking what appeared an underground blight and came out after dancing the spiral but were able to conceal their taint by dint of being elders, Wyrm magic etc particularly if they\u2019ve fallen to the Defiler Wyrm, they then spread their corruption throughout the their sept.<br /> <br /> The Tribe would have been scattered across the entirety of Caledonia, Faeroe Islands and Northern England, so word might not be that quick to spread and spirit messengers could be intercepted by banes.<br /> <br /> The new BSDs could easily infiltrate other camps and septs, murdering those too strong to corrupt and either luring or kidnapping other Howlers dragging them into the Spiral, at the same time corrupting kinfolk.<br /> <br /> Actually the Fianna would have been the heroes, eventually informed by fleeing Howler kinfolk, spirits possibly even their Fey allies, they travel by Moonbridge from Hibernia, attacking the BSDs and saving as many kinfolk as they could.<br /> <br /> This is why the Fianna honour their fallen brothers and sisters, they were just to late too save them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-12T11:38:48-07:00","id":1517797,"title":"","content":"My take was that the Fall of the White Howlers wasn\u2019t something Garou in general feel shame about. Sure it\u2019s unfortunate but it\u2019s more a cautionary tale than anything else. I think the idea of it being a personal shame is more a take from the Fianna in particular.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s pretty well established that the Fianna are sentimental, even amongst Garou (it\u2019s part of why they get along with the Fae so well). They\u2019re also famously family and kin-minded. I honestly don\u2019t think there has to be a rational reason for them to feel shame about the loss of the Howlers - I think it\u2019s a feeling more akin to Survivor\u2019s Guilt. Rationally we know that there wasn\u2019t actually any mistakes the Fianna made which caused the corruption of the Howlers. But I think that ever since it happened the Fianna have beat themselves up over it - they were our neighbors and our kin, why didn\u2019t we do anything? Why didn\u2019t we see the signs? We could have done something.<br /> <br /> (Also I think the Fianna have a fairly rosy memory of their relationship with the Howlers before the fall. I\u2019m sure that before the corruption there was a relationship with as much violence and machismo and bad blood as the Fianna still have with the Fenrir. But when the Howlers became corrupted they were transmuted into saints who never had done a wrong in their lives in the memories of Stag\u2019s Children).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-12T15:20:13-07:00","id":1517833,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1516631-rage-in-scotland?p=1517797#post1517797\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">(Also I think the Fianna have a fairly rosy memory of their relationship with the Howlers before the fall. I\u2019m sure that before the corruption there was a relationship with as much violence and machismo and bad blood as the Fianna still have with the Fenrir. But when the Howlers became corrupted they were transmuted into saints who never had done a wrong in their lives in the memories of Stag\u2019s Children).</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is an important point, many of the Garous customs and outlooks come from their human kinfolk tribes, the Fenrir from the Norse, Fianna from the celts and so on.<br /> <br /> So it\u2019s likely back in ancient days that the Picts would have warred with the Celts and their Garou so the Fianna probably left the Howlers to their territory and wouldn\u2019t have been welcome if they\u2019d interfered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27824-arcanus","name":"Arcanus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27824_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"148"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-07-18T04:01:39-07:00","id":1518234,"title":"","content":"From the Changeling perspective (with some Mage thrown in), there is the <i>Isle of the Mighty</i> volume, of which a third is about Scotland.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26145-babi_gog","name":"babi_gog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26145_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"94"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}